.TH AURORA-SEP-CRYPTENROLL 8 "2026" "aurora-sep-userspace" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
aurora-sep-cryptenroll \- unlock a LUKS2 volume with the Mac's Secure Enclave
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B aurora-sep-cryptenroll
[\fB\-\-wipe\-slot=sep\fR] [\fB\-\-unlock\-key\-file=\fIFILE\fR] \fIDEVICE\fR
.br
.B aurora-sep-cryptenroll
\fB\-\-remove\fR | \fB\-\-test\fR | \fB\-\-status\fR \fIDEVICE\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
Adds a keyslot to a LUKS2 volume whose secret is sealed by this Mac's Secure
Enclave (SEP), like
.B systemd-cryptenroll \-\-tpm2-device=auto
does with a TPM. The secret is stored in an \fBaurora-sep\fR LUKS2 token,
encrypted with an AES key that the kernel's \fBtrusted\fR key type seals
through the \fBapple_sep\fR driver. Only the same Mac, running Linux under the
same SEP OS identity, can unseal it.
.PP
cryptsetup and systemd-cryptsetup try the token automatically through
.IR libcryptsetup-token-aurora-sep.so .
If the SEP refuses, they ask for a passphrase as usual.
.PP
Enrolling needs an existing passphrase, which is kept. Removing never leaves
a volume without a keyslot that is not bound to the SEP.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-unlock\-key\-file= FILE
Existing passphrase or key file that authorizes the enrolment. Without it the
\fBPASSWORD\fR environment variable is used, else a prompt on the terminal.
.TP
.B \-\-wipe\-slot=sep
After the new enrolment is proven, remove older SEP enrolments.
.TP
.B \-\-remove
Remove every SEP keyslot and token.
.TP
.B \-\-test
Unseal each SEP token and check that it opens its keyslot. Nothing is activated.
.TP
.B \-\-status
List SEP tokens, their keyslots and how many other keyslots exist.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B AURORA_SEP_WAIT
Seconds the token plugin waits for the SEP key store at boot (default 60).
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
aurora-sep-cryptenroll /dev/nvme0n1p11
echo "data UUID=... none luks" >> /etc/crypttab
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cryptsetup (8),
.BR systemd-cryptenroll (1),
.BR crypttab (5),
.BR keyctl (1)
